+## Criterion added — round 24 (metadata is not missing evidence)
+
+| ID | Admit only when | Reject or limit when | Motivating lesson |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| KQ35 (qualifies, does not supersede, KQ32 and KQ34) | A refusal of an audited repository because it declares a promisor or partial-clone transport — `extensions.partialclone`, `remote.<name>.promisor`, `remote.<name>.partialclonefilter`, or a `.promisor` marker beside its pack — is credited only when the run establishes, per required object (commit, tree, blob, or committed report), that the object is not present in the local object store; the declaration is evidence of a transport arrangement, never evidence of absence. Availability is measured locally under the unchanged isolation (`--no-lazy-fetch`, `--no-replace-objects`, log and signature suppression, the git deadline) and the outcome follows the measured state: when every required object is local, the audit returns the same substantive finding as an otherwise identical full clone (clean exit 0, or the same failed exit 1 with the same finding kinds); when a required object is unavailable, it returns could-not-evaluate exit 2 with a pinned reason naming the unavailable object and its repository, never a clean or failed verdict; and a citation that cannot be resolved in a promisor-backed repository is unknown (exit 2) unless the same object is found locally in another audited repository, which is then cited. The rule is credited only through a paired population: every promisor-marked case has an otherwise identical full-clone twin on the same row, and a blanket-refusal mutant — the repaired auditor with the unconditional promisor refusal restored, metadata alone — is killed by those paired rows on grid exit 1 with the failed rows pasted (a grid that could not evaluate kills nothing; KQ25). | The presence of promisor metadata is treated as proof that evidence is missing, or absence is inferred from a declaration instead of measured; a correct complete-state workflow is refused and the refusal is excused because it was disclosed or written down as a declared trade (the round-23 closure: `corpus/lessons/round-23.md:83-84` declares that partial clones are refused "even when every cited object is local", and `:206-209` declares that T01 and F23 are refused with exit 2 "where frozen gave a verdict"); the refusal is charged to the metadata instead of the missing object, so no unavailable object is ever named; a missing object is fetched on demand instead of measured (S099); exit 2 is presented as a clean or failed verdict; a complete clone's own verdict changes because metadata is present; the blanket-refusal mutant is claimed killed by a grid that could not evaluate, by an unrelated row, or by the refusal rows that both arms share (mutual blindness: F23 refuses for a genuinely missing blob in both arms, so only the complete-local paired row can kill the blanket refusal); or KQ32's trusted-repository limit is lifted by this row. | `corpus/lessons/round-23.md` §Declared tradeoffs and §Declared trades (the round-23 closure) and M1/M6; `corpus/SOURCES.md` S099; `tests/report-audit/repo-state-grid.py` rows T01/F23/F27/C09/C10; `tests/report-audit/isolation-mutants.py` M14/M22/M27/M29; `.ufo/waves/round-24/preregistration.json` expected_before |
